Cloud radius 24-41, camera radius 61. The ring at 24-41 is closer than board center, so foreground. In portrait, they appear huge. We could scale the whole cloud group down in portrait by 0.55 and push radius out by +25. But the clouds are individual scene objects, not a group. We can update in applyViewport:
```
const cloudScale = portrait ? 0.55 : 1;
clouds.forEach(c => { c.scale.setScalar(cloudScale); });
```
and adjust their orbital radius? userData.r used in animation. We can store baseR and in animation use data.r * (portrait?1.5:1). Or set position radius in applyViewport and animation. The animation loop uses data.r, so modify data.r when portrait changes. Simpler: in applyViewport, set `c.scale.setScalar(portrait ? 0.6 : 1)`. Even if in front, smaller clouds won't block as much. The huge foreground cloud at bottom left is from second loop y negative, radius 16-36, very close. Scaling 0.6 helps.

Also for portrait, maybe hide the lower ring (y negative) entirely to declutter. We can tag clouds `userData.layer=1` for first ring, `layer=2` for second. In applyViewport, set visible for lower ring only if !portrait. And scale first ring 0.55. This will clean up mobile.

But still board small. Could increase portrait FOV? No, lower FOV (telephoto) would make board larger? With fixed fit, board width fills frame regardless of FOV; lower FOV requires greater distance, perspective flattens. Board height relative to width depends on elevation. To make board taller, use higher elevation (more top-down) as discussed. For portrait, set polar 0.62 instead of 0.76. Then board height/width ~ cos(polar)/? Actually projected depth ratio ~ sin(elevation) = cos(polar). At polar 0.62, cos=0.814, so height ~0.8*width = 300px. That's much better. But top-down view may look less 3D. A compromise polar 0.68 => cos=0.777 => height ~280px. Let's try 0.66. The board will fill width 360 and height ~280, good for mobile. On desktop keep 0.86 (41° elevation) which looks nice.

Also camera target y for portrait should be different to center board. We'll test.

